BOTD HOCKEY COACHING AID — VERSION 3.3
Creator: Nicholas Heller
Release date: 2026-08-01

OVERVIEW

BOTD Hockey Coaching Aid is a standalone HTML5 tactics board for designing,
drawing, animating, saving, and presenting hockey plays. It is intended for a
Windows desktop browser with a mouse and for iPad use with touch or Apple
Pencil. The application is contained in one HTML file and does not require an
installation, account, external JavaScript library, server, or internet
connection.

Nicholas Heller is acknowledged in the HTML source and in this README as the
creator of the application, its product concept, coaching workflow, and feature
direction.

PACKAGE CONTENTS

1. BOTD_Hockey_Coaching_Aid_v3.3.html
   The complete standalone application. BOTD artwork, rink graphics, audio
   synthesis, sample rosters, and the three default sample plays are embedded or
   generated within this file. No separate run-time assets are required.

2. BOTD_Hockey_Coaching_Aid_v3.3_Preview.png
   A 1920 x 1080 desktop preview of a whole-ice moving-entry turn. It shows the
   independent Skating and Puck action lanes, several simultaneous Move routes,
   a timed saucer pass, its orange release marker, and the release-time editor.

3. BOTD_Hockey_Coaching_Aid_v3.3_README.txt
   Setup instructions, Version 3.3 release notes, operating guidance,
   compatibility and storage notes, known limitations, verification details,
   and the complete cumulative change history.

RUNNING ON WINDOWS

1. Extract the ZIP archive to a normal folder.
2. Double-click BOTD_Hockey_Coaching_Aid_v3.3.html.
3. Open it with a current version of Microsoft Edge, Google Chrome, or Firefox.
4. A local file:/// address in the browser is expected. The application runs
   locally and does not need a web server.

Do not run the HTML directly from inside the ZIP archive. Extract it first so
browser storage, image uploads, and file exports behave more consistently.

RUNNING ON IPAD OR WITH APPLE PENCIL

The board uses standard Pointer Events and supports touch, Apple Pencil, mouse,
and trackpad input. For the most consistent iPad experience, place the same HTML
file on a static HTTPS website, open it in Safari, and use Add to Home Screen.
Opening a local HTML file directly from the Files app can depend on the file
provider and the installed iPadOS version.

VERSION 3.3 CHANGES

DUAL ACTION LANES

Each player may now have two independent action lanes in the same turn:

- Skating lane: Move
- Puck lane: Pass or Shoot

A Line switch remains exclusive and replaces both lanes for that player. The
turn may still contain several simultaneous Move actions, while one Pass or
Shoot event is shared across the turn.

This allows the puck carrier to:

- skate and pass during the same turn;
- skate and shoot during the same turn;
- retain possession before the selected release time;
- release the puck at a precise point on the skating route; and
- continue skating after the puck leaves the stick.

A moving receiver may also have a Move action in that turn. The pass endpoint is
calculated against the receiver's timed route so the puck leads the receiver to
an interception point instead of traveling to the receiver's original location.

TIMED PASS AND SHOT RELEASE

When a puck carrier has both a Move action and a Pass or Shoot action, Version
3.3 exposes an editable release time.

The release may be set in four ways:

- choose At start;
- choose At end;
- preview the turn and choose Set from playhead;
- drag the orange PASS or SHOT marker along the recorded skating route; or
- drag the release-time range control in the selected-player card.

The pass or shot dialog also includes a release-time control. A stationary
player releases at the start of the turn because no skating timeline exists for
that player.

Changing or replacing the passer's Move route automatically recalculates the
release source, pass timing, moving-receiver lead, bank eligibility, and puck
trajectory. Changing the receiver's Move route also recalculates the lead pass.

PASS-ON-THE-MOVE POSSESSION BEHAVIOR

During a timed pass:

- the carrier remains in possession until the release time;
- the puck is loose while traveling;
- the passer continues along the remaining Move route;
- a moving receiver continues along that receiver's own Move route;
- the receiver gains possession when an unobstructed pass arrives; and
- a blocked pass finishes loose at the calculated impact point.

Possession changes authored at the beginning of the turn still take priority.
Changing possession away from the scheduled passer removes an invalid Pass or
Shoot, as in earlier releases.

RESTRICTED BANK-PASS SELECTION

The direct pass is tested first. A bank pass is considered only when the direct
lane is obstructed.

For each candidate wall contact, Version 3.3 calculates:

- L1: distance from the passer's release point to the wall contact; and
- L2: distance from the wall contact to the receiver's predicted arrival point.

A candidate bank is eligible only when L2 is less than or equal to L1. Among
eligible, unobstructed candidates, the shortest total route is selected. If no
candidate satisfies that rule, the app retains the straight pass attempt.

When that retained straight pass intersects a player or goalie, it is recorded
as a blocked pass and the puck remains loose at the impact point. This preserves
the requested straight-pass fallback without allowing the puck to pass through
the blocker and attach incorrectly to the receiver.

For a moving receiver, L2 is measured to the calculated interception position,
not to the receiver's starting position.

BIDIRECTIONAL PLAYBACK SCRUBBING

The playback range control is now a true reversible playhead.

- Drag it to the right to move forward through the play.
- Drag it to the left to reconstruct earlier player and puck states.
- Player positions, line changes, possession, loose-puck location, pass and shot
  flight, puck spin, route traces, and goal visuals are recomputed from the
  authored turn data at the selected time.
- Beginning a scrub pauses active playback so the animation does not fight the
  pointer.
- Scrubbing across a recorded goal displays the correct GOAL! visual state but
  does not repeatedly replay the goal siren.
- Normal forward playback still triggers the siren at the recorded goal time.

CLEANER PLAYBACK VIEW

Dotted player-selection outlines and turn-selection outlines are hidden while
normal playback is running. They return when playback stops so the coach can
continue editing. The outlines are also hidden while the timeline thumb is
actively being dragged.

CONTINUED VERSION 3.2 AND EARLIER BEHAVIOR

Version 3.3 retains:

- whole ice as the default fresh-board view;
- portrait north-south half ice;
- the BOTD center-ice tag and playbook image;
- the BOTD Playbook and three default core plays;
- blue/white DAWGS and white/red WILDCATS starter teams;
- full funny-name rosters, captain and alternate profiles;
- loose goalie rebounds;
- goal detection, in-net puck containment, red goal lamps, GOAL! visual, and
  local browser-generated siren;
- rounded-corner rim-around shot paths;
- spinning shots and saucer passes;
- Ahead; erase behind as the default route mode;
- collapsible left, right, and timeline panels;
- local playbook saving and portable JSON import/export; and
- mouse, touch, trackpad, and Apple Pencil input.

BASIC WORKFLOW

1. Open Teams to edit team names, colors, rink view, center logo, and playbook
   image.
2. Open Roster to edit number, name, position, goalie status, captain status,
   alternate status, and active-bench membership.
3. Select Load active players to benches after changing the active roster.
4. Select a bench token to place that player on the ice. Drag the rink tokens to
   establish the starting formation.
5. Place the loose puck or assign possession to any on-ice player.
6. Select New turn and include the players participating in that turn.
7. Assign each participating player's Skating lane action.
8. Assign the one puck event for that turn, when needed.
9. For a moving passer or shooter, set the orange release marker or release-time
   slider.
10. Select Done turn and repeat for later turns.
11. Press Play to review the sequence, or drag the playback slider in either
   direction to inspect a particular moment.
12. Press Stop at any point and begin a new turn there to branch from that state.
13. Name the playbook and play, then save locally or export JSON backups.

PASSING ON THE MOVE — STEP BY STEP

1. Give the intended passer possession.
2. Select New turn.
3. Include the passer, receiver, and any other moving players or defenders.
4. Select the passer and record a Move route.
5. Select the receiver and record the receiver's Move route, when applicable.
6. Record the routes of other players acting in the turn.
7. Select the passer and choose Pass.
8. Select the intended receiver.
9. Choose Flat pass or Saucer pass.
10. Set the release time in the dialog, or add the pass and then drag the orange
    marker on the passer's route.
11. Preview the turn with the selected-player preview playhead when more precise
    timing is needed.
12. Select Done turn.
13. Press Play or scrub the timeline. The puck stays with the carrier until the
    release point, then leads the moving receiver.

MOVING SHOT — STEP BY STEP

1. Give the shooter possession.
2. Start a turn and record the shooter's Move route.
3. With that player still selected, choose Shoot.
4. Select the target and power.
5. Set the release time in the shot dialog or with the orange SHOT marker.
6. Finish the turn.
7. The skater moves with the puck, releases at the selected time, and continues
   skating while the shot follows the Version 3.2/3.1 shot and goal physics.

TIMELINE SCRUBBING AND BRANCHING

- Dragging the slider automatically stops active playback.
- The slider can be moved repeatedly forward and backward without modifying the
  authored turns.
- To branch, leave the playhead at the desired time, select a player, and choose
  Start turn here or Turn from playhead.
- A branch turn captures player locations, bench state, puck location, and
  possession at that playhead time.
- The GOAL! panel may appear while scrubbing through the short celebration
  window. Audio is reserved for normal forward playback.

SHOT, GOAL, BLOCK, AND RIM-AROUND WORKFLOW

1. Give an on-ice player possession.
2. Start or continue a turn and select that player.
3. Select Shoot.
4. Select a target on the rink.
5. Choose power from 1 through 10.
6. For a moving shooter, select the release point.
7. To score, aim so the calculated path crosses the goal line through the
   opening between the posts. The puck finishes inside the net.
8. Complete the turn and press Play. At the recorded goal time, the red lamp,
   GOAL! panel, rink pulse, and browser-generated siren activate.
9. For a rim-around, choose a target at a shallow angle near the boards. The app
   follows the rounded boundary while modeled energy remains.
10. If the path intersects a skater or goalie before entering the net, the shot
    is blocked, performs a short rebound, and ends loose.

DRAWING CONTROLS

- Select tool: select and reposition setup objects when editing is allowed.
- Draw tool: draw with mouse, touch, or Apple Pencil.
- Erase tool: cross a freehand stroke to remove the entire stroke.
- Preset colors, a custom color, and adjustable line width are available.
- Undo, redo, and clear-drawing controls affect coaching marks independently of
  the animation timeline.

TURN, POSSESSION, AND ROUTE CONTROLS

- Several Move actions may run simultaneously in one turn.
- A player may combine Move with Pass or Shoot.
- One Pass or Shoot event is permitted in a turn.
- Line switch is exclusive for the player being changed.
- Possession may be assigned to any on-ice player, stolen from another player,
  or released so the puck remains loose.
- A possession change is stored as a turn event and reproduced in playback.
- Canonical turns may be reused or copied and edited.
- Route modes are Show all, Ahead; erase behind, and Hidden.
- The left setup panel, right Play Animator panel, and turn timeline may be
  collapsed to create more rink space.
- Half ice is oriented north-south and fills the available portrait space.

TEAM PLAYBOOK AND FILES

- Plays are stored locally in browser localStorage when available.
- Save play updates the current saved play. Save copy creates another entry.
- Export downloads one portable .hockeyplay.json file.
- Export playbook downloads the complete named playbook as JSON.
- Import accepts compatible play and playbook JSON from earlier builds.
- Clearing browser site data can remove local saves. Export important work.
- The three sample plays are normal playbook entries and may be changed or
  deleted by the user.

STORAGE MIGRATION

Version 3.3 uses the following new storage namespaces:

- botdHockeyCoachingAid.session.v3_3
- botdHockeyCoachingAid.playbook.v3_3

When a Version 3.3 record does not yet exist, the app can normalize a Version
3.2, Version 3.1, Version 3.0, Version 0.2.1, Version 0.2, or Version 0.1 session
or playbook record. Existing playbook entries are retained, and missing BOTD
core samples are appended without replacing an entry with the same identifier
or title.

Older storage records are not overwritten. Version 3.3 autosaves later work
under its own keys. Portable JSON files from earlier releases may also be
imported. Export important playbooks before clearing browser site data.

PRIVACY AND NETWORK USE

- The application makes no network requests.
- There are no analytics, accounts, trackers, advertisements, or cloud APIs.
- Uploaded logos are resized and processed locally in the browser.
- Default BOTD images are embedded inside the HTML file.
- Plays remain in the selected browser unless the user exports them.

COMPATIBILITY

Recommended desktop browsers:

- Microsoft Edge, current release
- Google Chrome, current release
- Mozilla Firefox, current release

The same standards-based code path is used by Safari. Browser handling of local
HTML files and localStorage can differ, especially on mobile devices.

VERSION 3.3 TECHNICAL NOTES AND KNOWN LIMITATIONS

- A player may have one Skating lane action and one Puck lane action in a turn.
  A second Pass or Shoot must be authored in the next turn.
- Catch-and-shoot, pass-pass, or touch-pass sequences use successive turns.
- A release marker exists only when the puck carrier has a recorded Move route.
  Stationary Pass and Shoot actions release at the start of the turn.
- Moving-receiver interception is solved iteratively from the passer's timed
  release position, puck speed, route length, and receiver route. The result is
  tuned for coaching visualization rather than laboratory ballistics.
- Moving-player pass obstruction is sampled at short intervals along the puck
  route. Extremely brief contacts between samples are theoretically possible.
- A retained straight pass that is blocked stops at an approximated contact
  point and leaves the puck loose. The app does not yet model a detailed pass
  deflection, stick angle, possession battle, or whistle after that contact.
- Pass bank selection uses a single wall contact and the L2 <= L1 rule. Passes do
  not yet ride the curved corners in the way that rim-around shots can.
- Shot collision checks against moving skaters use the authored turn state and
  release-time setup; they are not a continuous rigid-body solver.
- Goal physics do not separately simulate posts, crossbars, net mesh
  deformation, disputed goals, video review, or pucks wedged outside the net.
- Rounded shot boards use a densely sampled geometric boundary rather than a
  full material-contact simulation.
- Puck speed, energy retention, spin, rebound distance, and board travel are
  tuned for readable tactics instruction.
- A blocked shot or pass becomes loose. Recovery, a goalie cover, or another
  possession outcome is authored in the next turn.
- Web Audio depends on browser policy and device support. Muted tabs, silent
  mode, or unsupported browsers may suppress sound while visual goal feedback
  remains.
- The captain role is limited to one player per team. Multiple alternates are
  permitted.

RELEASE VERIFICATION

The Version 3.3 standalone build was exercised in desktop Chromium at
1920 x 1080 for:

- Version 3.3 identity, whole-ice default, Ahead; erase behind default, DAWGS and
  WILDCATS defaults, BOTD Playbook name, and three core sample plays;
- simultaneous Move actions plus a timed Pass for the same puck carrier;
- simultaneous Move actions plus a timed Shoot for the same puck carrier;
- pass and shot source positions taken from the carrier's route at release;
- moving-receiver lead calculation at the puck arrival time;
- dialog release controls, selected-player release slider, quick release
  choices, preview playhead, and direct dragging of the orange release marker;
- continuous release-slider dragging without replacing the active range input;
- preservation of the selected player while the release marker is dragged;
- possession before release, loose puck in flight, and receiver possession on
  an unobstructed arrival;
- eligible bank selection only when L2 <= L1;
- straight-pass fallback when no eligible bank exists;
- blocked straight-pass impact and loose-puck completion;
- repeated forward and backward timeline dragging with reconstructed player and
  puck state;
- automatic pause when scrubbing starts and cleanup when the pointer is
  released;
- dotted selection outlines hidden during playback and restored after Stop;
- moving-shot goal timing, in-net completion, goal visual while scrubbing, and
  suppression of repeated siren audio during scrubbing;
- all three default sample plays in whole-ice and half-ice modes;
- disabled playback controls before a completed turn;
- normalization of Version 3.2-style actions that do not contain release-time
  fields;
- JavaScript syntax, runtime exceptions, and console errors; and
- PNG preview dimensions and ZIP compressed-file integrity.

All listed release checks passed in the release environment.

COMPLETE VERSION HISTORY

VERSION 0.1 — INITIAL STANDALONE HTML5 MVP

- Created a standalone single-file HTML application for desktop browsers.
- Added whole-ice and half-ice rink backgrounds.
- Added editable team names, primary colors, and trim colors.
- Added uploadable center-ice and Team Playbook images.
- Added editable rosters with optional number, name, position, goalie status,
  and active-player selection.
- Added distinct skater and goalie tokens.
- Added team benches and placement of active players onto the rink.
- Added explicit puck-possession state.
- Added sequential Move, Pass, Shoot, and Line switch actions.
- Captured Move timing from the speed used to draw each skating path.
- Added global playback-speed controls.
- Added flat and saucer passes with automatic duration and receiver attachment.
- Added direct-lane obstruction checks and best-available single-bank pass
  routing around players, goalies, boards, and nets.
- Made shots stop at the first intersected player; goalie contact attached the
  puck to the goalie in this original release.
- Added freehand multicolor drawing, adjustable width, undo, redo, whole-stroke
  erasing, and clear-all.
- Added browser-local Team Playbook saving and portable JSON import/export.
- Added mouse, touch, and Apple Pencil Pointer Events support.

VERSION 0.2 — TURN-BASED ANIMATION AND WORKSPACE REDESIGN

- Added two complete 20-player sample rosters with funny names, numbers,
  positions, and two goalies per team.
- Hid player labels on rink tokens by default.
- Added collapsible left setup drawer, right Play Animator drawer, and animation
  timeline.
- Reworked half ice into a north-south portrait presentation.
- Added a turn builder for simultaneous player actions.
- Allowed multiple players to Move during the same turn while a Pass or Shoot
  ran.
- Added moving-receiver lead calculation for passes.
- Added Stop-at-playhead editing and alternate outcome branching.
- Added canonical reusable turns such as T1, T2, T3, T1, T2, T4.
- Added Reuse and Copy & edit controls for turns.
- Added route modes for Show all, Ahead; erase behind, and Hidden.
- Allowed a loose puck to be dragged to a starting location before animation.
- Reserved incoming line-change players to prevent duplicate simultaneous
  substitutions.
- Added normalization of Version 0.1 JSON data into the turn format.

VERSION 0.2.1 — POSSESSION PATCH AND LARGER HALF ICE

- Allowed possession to change while a turn was being built.
- Allowed any on-ice player to take or steal the puck.
- Added Make loose so the puck could remain unpossessed between turns.
- Made a possession-only turn valid.
- Enabled Pass and Shoot immediately for the new carrier.
- Removed an invalid Pass or Shoot automatically if possession changed.
- Stored possession changes as turn events for playback, copying, reuse,
  playbook saving, and JSON export/import.
- Recalculated portrait half ice from live workspace dimensions and expanded it
  to the maximum practical height.
- Refit half ice after window resizing and drawer changes.

VERSION 3.0 — BOTD BRANDING, NAMED PLAYBOOKS, AND PUCK PHYSICS

- Renamed the product BOTD Hockey Coaching Aid.
- Acknowledged Nicholas Heller as creator in source notes and metadata.
- Added the supplied BOTD default center-ice and playbook artwork.
- Added custom playbook naming and name-aware export behavior.
- Changed the starting teams to blue/white DAWGS and white/red WILDCATS.
- Added spinning shot and saucer-puck visuals.
- Added timed shot trajectories, motion streaks, skater block rebounds, goalie
  saves, flat-board reflections, angle-dependent energy retention,
  power-dependent travel, and multi-board ricochet support.
- Extended collision checks over post-board shot paths.
- Removed the player-deletion confirmation prompt.
- Changed release packaging to a TXT README with the full cumulative history.

VERSION 3.1 — LOOSE GOALIE REBOUNDS, ROUNDED RIMS, AND CORE PLAYBOOK

- Changed goalie shot contact from automatic possession to a loose-puck block
  with a visible rebound.
- Added rounded-board tracks and corner-following rim-around shot physics.
- Kept steep-angle impacts as reflected banks.
- Added captain and alternate-captain toggles, persistence, and selection-card
  badges.
- Made Ahead; erase behind the default route mode for new boards and samples.
- Named the default collection BOTD Playbook.
- Seeded three three-turn core plays: whole-ice 5v5, portrait half-ice 5v5, and
  whole-ice 2v1.
- Added goalie trapezoid diagonal markings to whole and half ice.
- Visibly grayed and disabled unavailable playback controls until a turn was
  completed.
- Added Version 3.1 storage migration and cumulative release validation.

VERSION 3.2 — NET-CONTAINED GOALS, GOAL SIREN, AND RINK CORRECTIONS

- Added goal-line and goal-mouth detection for recorded shots.
- Stopped scoring shots inside the net so they no longer bounce from the end
  boards back onto the ice.
- Preserved pre-goal skater and goalie collision priority and loose rebounds.
- Added an illuminated red goal lamp, rink flash, scoring-team GOAL! panel,
  browser-generated goal siren, optional vibration, and timeline event tracking.
- Corrected goalkeeper restricted-area markings to NHL Rule 1.8 proportions:
  22 feet at the goal line and 28 feet at the end boards.
- Made Whole ice the default view for fresh sessions and new boards.
- Added Version 3.2 storage keys, Version 3.1 playbook migration, goal metadata,
  updated validation, and cumulative release documentation.

VERSION 3.3 — PASSING ON THE MOVE AND REVERSIBLE PLAYHEAD

- Replaced the single per-player action restriction with independent Skating and
  Puck lanes.
- Allowed Move plus Pass and Move plus Shoot for the same player in one turn.
- Added timed puck release from the carrier's recorded Move route.
- Added At start, At end, Set from playhead, range-slider, and draggable orange
  release-marker controls.
- Added a turn-preview playhead for choosing a release moment before committing
  the turn.
- Recalculated the pass source from the moving passer's timed position.
- Recalculated the endpoint to lead a moving receiver at the puck arrival time.
- Kept possession with the passer until release, made the puck loose in flight,
  and allowed both skaters to continue moving independently.
- Added moving-player obstruction sampling for calculated pass routes.
- Restricted automatic bank passes to candidates whose second leg is no longer
  than the first leg.
- Retained a straight pass when no bank satisfies that rule.
- Made an obstructed straight-pass fallback stop at the blocker and finish loose.
- Added true forward-and-backward playback-slider scrubbing with complete state
  reconstruction.
- Prevented the goal siren from repeating while the user scrubs across a goal,
  while retaining the visual goal state.
- Hid dotted player and turn-selection rings during playback and active
  scrubbing, restoring them after Stop.
- Added Version 3.3 browser-storage keys and migration from earlier sessions and
  playbooks.
- Updated the standalone package, 1920 x 1080 preview, TXT documentation,
  cumulative version history, and release validation.

END OF README
